The \code{discretion} modifier of a plan's pricing: a visible limit that quietly takes a slightly worse price when one comes within reach.

The visible limit rests where the pricing rule put it. When the other side comes within \code{points} of it, UBI first reduces or cancels the resting order and then takes \code{quantity}, by default all that still rests, with a limit two ticks past the other side's touch but never past the visible price plus \code{points}. A buy resting at 1000 with 0.25 of discretion takes an offer of 1000.20. It goes beside the one pricing rule, as \code{OrderPart$new(pricing = ..., discretion = ...)}. It needs one visible limit, so UBI refuses it on a stop with \code{discretion_needs_limit} and with any execution other than all at once with \code{discretion_not_sliced}.
